            "text": "| Comparison | $x$ values |\n|---|---|\n| Equal heights | $-1,\\ 2$ |\n| $P$ below $L$ | $-1<x<2$ |\n| $P$ above $L$ | $x<-1$ or $x>2$ |",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "text": "| Comparison | $x$ values |\n|---|---|\n| Equal heights | $-1,\\ 2$ |\n| $P$ below $L$ | $x<-1$ or $x>2$ |\n| $P$ above $L$ | $-1<x<2$ |",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Use actual crossing abscissas and compare two function heights at a shared x to obtain equality and both strict inequality solution sets, including endpoint exclusions.",
        "source_solution": "The intersections are (-1,1) and (2,4), so equality holds at x=-1,2. Between them P is below L, confirmed by heights 0 and 2 at x=0. Outside, P is above L, confirmed by heights 4 and 0 at x=-2 and 9 and 5 at x=3. Thus less-than is -1<x<2 and greater-than is x<-1 or x>2. At crossings heights are equal, so strict comparisons exclude both endpoints. Independently (x+1)(x-2)=x^2-x-2 verifies the full-domain behavior.",
        "unit_reason": "The equality and two inequalities are one coherent graphical comparison partitioning the same real line. A complete table retains all three source tasks and prevents independent answer-slot treatment or formula-only root selection.",
        "concept_blocks": [9],
        "limitations": ["The supplied graphs test interpretation, not independently plotting both functions.", "Selecting a complete table does not demonstrate independently writing interval notation."],
