      "intent": {
        "task": "Use six actual pairs of graphs to determine all real intersection abscissas, preserving signed source coefficients, proper full-equation normalization, two nonrational root pairs, and the no-real-intersection case.",
        "source_solution": "A: y=x^2 and y=-3x+4 meet at x=-4,1. B: y=x^2 and y=2x+5 meet at x=1+-sqrt(6), approximately -1.44949,3.44949. C: y=x^2 is above y=-2x-5 for all x because their difference is (x+1)^2+4, so there are no real roots. D: divide the full equation by 2 to get y=x^2 and y=x/2+3/2; roots -1,3/2. E: divide every term by 3 to get y=x^2 and y=-2x/3+5/3; roots -5/3,1. F: divide every term by 5 to get y=x^2 and y=-12x/5+2; completing the square gives x=(-6+-sqrt(86))/5, approximately -3.05472,0.65472. The offered true values differ from exact roots by less than 0.005, comfortably inside the visible 0.05 acceptance. Intersections are read horizontally, not by their heights or separate axis intercepts.",
        "unit_reason": "A complete six-row table forms one coherent graph-to-root correspondence, retaining every source equation and comparison case. Reusing one framed graph sheet keeps each pair's frame intact while avoiding six repetitive root-slot items. Recognizing a completed result is the response unit; no quadratic-formula blanks replace the graphical method.",
